# What never reaches a container build. See deploy/jetson/Dockerfile.
#
# The first entry is the one that matters. `.env` on a developer's machine holds real API keys,
# the Dockerfile copies the repository root, and a copied secret does not stop existing when a
# later layer deletes it: it is in the build cache on whatever machine did the build.
#
# The `**/` matters more than it looks. Docker matches these against the whole path
# relative to the build context, so a bare `.env` covers the repository root and misses
# `deploy/jetson/.env`, which is the one file the compose file tells a Jetson user to put
# a provider key in.
**/.env
**/.env.*

.git
.gitattributes
.github

# Virtualenvs are the host's, built for the host's platform and its interpreter. Copying one
# into an arm64 image would be copying Windows or x86 binaries that nothing can run.
.venv
.venv*/

# Artifacts, not source.
runs/
dist/
build/
*.egg-info/
MUJOCO_LOG.TXT
.coverage
htmlcov/

# Caches: large, host specific, and rebuilt on demand.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/

# Not read by the wheel build. `hatch_build.py` reads README.md and nothing else, and the
# adapters read their own. Everything here is either large (docs/assets carries the recordings)
# or irrelevant to a running container.
docs/
web/
tests/
bridge/
scripts/
CHANGELOG.md
CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md
CONTRIBUTING.md
SECURITY.md
LAUNCH.md
PLAN.md
vercel.json
.pre-commit-config.yaml

# Upstream assets are never vendored (docs/licenses.md), and a debugging copy beside the
# checkout must not become a layer of a published image either.
*.onnx
*.pt
*.stl
robot_walk.xml
